            <div xml:id="d171">

                <pb facs="0235" n="153" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg153"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 738.3915/43.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Santo
                                Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-01-14"><hi rendition="#italic">January 14,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 25.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The minister for foreign affairs told me
                    this morning that the Haitian Government had refused to accept Santo Domingo’s
                    proposition for a joint commission as outlined in my last cable. A commission
                    from Haiti is now at the frontier with the Haitian minister to Santo Domingo.
                    Santo Domingo has also sent two commissioners. These commissioners and the
                    Haitian commission are expected to arrive at some satisfactory <hi
                        rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi>. The Dominican troops are still at
                    the scene, but are expected to be withdrawn very soon.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William W.
                    Russell.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
